														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3907819">
									

<p>Eh. This is kind of all over the place.</p>
<p>Not only do I have to agree with TL333s that this comes across as more prose than a clinical SCP article, but your characters here fall rather flat. SCP-4000 should ideally portray some kind of character. Even if they are not Satan himself, I'd imagine that the leader of a prehistoric/mythical army would be rather charismatic. Instead, this comes across as rather wooden, with choppy sentences that boil down to exposition. If this is 4000's attempt to win over the Foundation, they have utterly failed there.</p>
<p>That aside, you try to plot to weave several other bits of Foundation lore here into a consistent mythology and I don't think it gels as well as it might. I don't buy the Daevites locking themselves in a book as a means to escape the End, nor do I buy SCP-2000 as being created by the Clay. Nor SCP-682 as being a magical experiment.</p>
<p>On the whole, its an ambitious article, sure, but I feel in its ambition it sort of collapses in on itself, and crucial parts that are needed to carry the narrative are not there.</p>
<p>-1.</p>

							</div>

								
					
